// A payload-free enum variant written BARE in a pattern is a *binding*, not the variant: it matches
// every value, so `String => …` on a `Type` scrutinee answers "string" for every case and silently
// kills every arm below it. Nothing in the source looks wrong — a payload-carrying variant
// (`Type.List(inner)`) is call-shaped and needs no qualification, so the author has no cue that the
// payload-free one does. E0067 names the arm and the spelling it meant (`Type.String`); E0066 then
// reports each arm the catch-all swallowed, so the whole class is caught at the first wrong arm
// rather than at a wrong value far downstream.
//
// The hint stands on its own: `lone` has only ONE arm, so nothing is unreachable and E0066 is
// silent — but a bare `String` arm on a `Type` scrutinee is already the bug, and E0067 says so.
// expect: error E0067 at 26:9
// expect: error E0066 at 27:9
// expect: error E0066 at 28:9
// expect: error E0066 at 29:9
// expect: error E0067 at 35:9
// expect: exit 1
enum Type {
    String;
    Int;
    Bool;
    List(inner: string);
}

fn describe(ty: Type): string {
    return match ty {
        String => "string",
        Int => "int",
        Bool => "bool",
        _ => "other",
    }
}

fn lone(ty: Type): string {
    return match ty {
        String => "string",
    }
}

echo describe(Type.Int)
echo lone(Type.Bool)
